user_prompt:
Brand: Dior (category: Fashion, Apparel & Footwear)
Topic from the client: repair and reuse

You are continuing an idea development worksheet for one campaign for this brand on this topic. Four candidate campaign directions have already been written:

[
  {
    "id": "C1",
    "title": "The Flaw Collection",
    "core_idea": "A provocative couture show built on damage: moth holes, torn hems, worn elbows and faded creases from real clients' beloved pieces. Designers reinterpret each flaw as a deliberate couture signature, and the original owners attend the show to see their pieces reborn.",
    "brand_connection": "It uses Dior's runway theatre and creative-director authorship to claim that the most beautiful thing in fashion is a garment that has been lived in.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "High cultural impact, press-worthy and shareable.",
      "Emotionally resonant, because real people's stories sit at the centre.",
      "Positions Dior as bold on a topic where luxury is usually timid."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Risk of seeming to romanticise damage or devalue expensive pieces.",
      "A one-off spectacle with limited sustained behaviour change.",
      "Logistically complex, with client consent and creative-direction buy-in required."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C2",
    "title": "The Extra Inch",
    "core_idea": "Inside well-made couture garments, hidden seam allowances give the piece room to be taken in, let out and re-fitted over decades. The campaign reveals this concealed inch as Dior's quiet promise of a long life. It treats the garment as something designed to change with its owner, and it frames repair, refitting and re-gifting as the 'next inch' of the story.",
    "brand_connection": "It is rooted in a true piece of couture construction and in Dior's obsession with fit, from the New Look's architecture to the atelier. It turns an invisible technical detail into an ownable brand symbol of longevity, without a sustainability lecture.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Grounded in a real product truth, so it is credible and hard for competitors to copy.",
      "Highly platformable: the inch works as a visual device, a unit of measure, a film motif and a service name.",
      "Reframes luxury as a long-term relationship with a garment, not a purchase event.",
      "Works for both first owners (refits) and later owners (reuse)."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "The 'built to last' claim must be backed by real design standards and service capacity, or it risks greenwashing accusations.",
      "Seam allowances matter less for leather goods, shoes and accessories, so the metaphor needs stretching.",
      "Technical detail could feel dry if the storytelling is not emotional enough."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C3",
    "title": "Second Fitting",
    "core_idea": "A house-certified pre-owned programme in which returned Dior pieces are authenticated, restored and re-fitted by the atelier for a new owner. Each piece carries a numbered label recording its previous wearers. Provenance becomes the new status symbol, and being 'owner number two' is positioned as a mark of taste.",
    "brand_connection": "It uses Dior's archive culture and authenticity authority to own the resale conversation, and it turns lineage into luxury currency.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Directly addresses reuse with a tangible new offer.",
      "Lets Dior control quality, pricing and brand perception in the secondhand market.",
      "Creates a new entry point for younger audiences."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Could cannibalise new sales or dilute exclusivity if not tightly curated.",
      "It is a business-model decision, not only a campaign idea, so it needs significant internal alignment.",
      "Visible previous-owner history may not suit every client's privacy preferences."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C4",
    "title": "Hands That Remember",
    "core_idea": "Put the repairers, not the designers, in the spotlight. Each repair is credited by first name and shown in intimate close-up. The campaign celebrates the atelier people who restore, re-hem, re-line and revive, positioning repair as the highest form of couture skill.",
    "brand_connection": "It builds on Dior's craft mystique and the legacy of the Petites Mains, and it makes human skill the core of the brand's sustainability story.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Emotionally warm and beautiful to film.",
      "Gives internal talent recognition and supports employer branding.",
      "Easy to execute across editorial, social and in-store."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Craft-hero storytelling is a well-worn luxury trope, so it risks being forgettable.",
      "It tells people about repair but gives them little new to do or experience.",
      "Weak on reuse and secondhand."
    ]
  }
]

The selection section is already complete:

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction rooted in a real, verifiable product truth: the hidden seam allowance in well-made couture. That makes the longevity promise credible, ownable and hard for competitors to copy, which matters on a topic where luxury is often accused of greenwashing.",
      "It covers the whole topic. Refitting and repair serve first owners, and re-gifting and reuse serve later owners. C1 and C4 each cover only one side, and C3 covers only resale.",
      "It is highly platformable. The 'inch' can be a visual device, a unit of measure, a film motif, a service name and a product-design standard. This gives the campaign a long life beyond a single launch, unlike a one-off spectacle.",
      "It reframes luxury as a long-term relationship with a garment rather than a purchase event. This fits Dior's obsession with fit and the atelier, and it avoids a sustainability lecture, so it protects brand desirability.",
      "It gives people something to do: book a refit, take a piece in or let it out, or pass it on. This turns the idea into behaviour and service, not only a message.",
      "Its main risks can be managed. The claim can be backed by a published construction standard and atelier capacity. The metaphor can be stretched to leather goods and shoes through equivalents such as resoling, re-edging and re-strapping, framed as 'the next inch'. Emotional storytelling about real owners and real refits can offset the dryness of the technical detail."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It is bold and press-worthy, but it is a one-off spectacle with little sustained behaviour change and no ongoing service or offer for repair and reuse. It also risks seeming to romanticise damage and devalue expensive pieces, which could clash with Dior's craft and quality positioning. It depends on client consent and creative-director buy-in, so it carries high execution risk for a narrower payoff. Its best element, real owners' emotional stories, can be borrowed into C2."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "It gives a tangible reuse offer, but it is a business-model decision more than a campaign idea. It needs heavy internal alignment and risks cannibalising new sales and diluting exclusivity. Visible previous-owner history raises privacy concerns for clients. It also addresses resale only and does little for repair or the first owner's relationship with the piece. It works better as a later extension of C2, where 'owner number two' becomes the next inch."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "It is warm and beautiful to film and supports employer branding, but celebrating craft heroes is a well-worn luxury trope and risks being forgettable. It tells people about repair without giving them anything new to do or experience, and it is weak on reuse and secondhand. It works better as a storytelling layer inside C2, where the atelier hands perform the refits."
      }
    ]
  }
}

Complete the remaining sections of the worksheet:

1. revisions_after_selection: changes made to the recommended idea after reviewing it, with the reason for each. Use an empty list if there are none.
2. final_proposal: the recommended idea written for a human reader (at most 150 words), plus exactly 3 open questions that would help the reader develop further ideas of their own.

Write in English. Return these sections as a single JSON object with this structure and nothing else:
{
  "revisions_after_selection": [{"change": "...", "reason": "..."}],
  "final_proposal": {"title": "...", "idea": "...", "follow_up_questions": ["...", "...", "..."]}
}

